a new report sheds light on the events  surrounding a controversial photo op by  president trump last year park police  helped clear  washington's lafayette park not long  before the president walked across it to  stand in front of a church  they were accused of abusing american  citizens to help president trump look  good  chris van cleef was there that day and  he joins us now chris good morning to  you what's in this report  well good morning the clearing of the  park and the area that is now known as  black lives matter plaza happened ahead  of a city-wide curfew and came as a  surprise to many of the people there  including other law enforcement now this  department of the interior inspector  general report found the decision to  move the crowd back was made  hours before u.s park police leadership  learned of the president's photo op  where he held up a bible  park police told the ig the decision was  made to move the crowd further from the  white house to allow a vendor to come in  to install security fencing around  lafayette park and that came after a  weekend where there was  violent unrest in the city we were there  in the middle of the crowd that  stretched for blocks from what we could  see this was  a peaceful demonstration the city was  under a 7 p.m curfew but the police  moved in aggressively about 30 minutes  early the report says there were three  announcements  to disperse we heard none of those our  first warning was the sound of  flashbangs  and officers in riot gear rushing our  location  the report references then attorney  general bill barr who was on scene  during part of the protests  saying to a park police commander are  these people still going to be here  when potus the president comes out now  this is what bill barr said  about that on face the nation last june  i gave the green light at two o'clock  obviously i didn't know  that the president was going to be  speaking later that day  now this report does challenge the  narrative that the white house had the  protesters cleared solely for a photo op  but it  is narrow in scope it was done by the  department of the interior it was  focused on the park police response it  did not include interviews with former  trump officials like bill barr  and of course there are lingering  questions including that decision to  move in  before the curfew during what appeared  to be a largely  peaceful protest gail all right chris  thank you very much
 

